    {
      "id": "term-eng-t126",
      "termNumber": "126",
      "engSourceId": "eng-t126-v3",
      "title": "slave",
      "sourceFile": "eng/json/018.content.json",
      "selectedBy": "passage",
      "text": {
        "status": "source",
        "sourceId": "eng-t126-v3",
        "html": "<p>A <b>servant</b> is a person who does work for someone else. Servants may work in the house or in the fields of their <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t90-v1\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">master</data>. Servants usually have a low position in the community.</p><p>If people do not pay a servant for his work and the servant does not have the freedom to leave his master when he wants, then we call the servant a <b>slave</b>. A slave is the property of a master. The master can sell the slave to someone else, just as the master can sell his other property.</p><p>Some masters treated slaves cruelly, but other masters were kind and could even treat their slaves as if they were part of the family.</p><p>In the Bible, the word for servant and slave is often the same. Sometimes translations use a different word for \"servant\" to show that this person serves others, but is not a slave.</p><p>Because a servant is someone who does work for someone else, storytellers in the Bible sometimes use the word \"servant\" when they talk about an official or military leader. These officials or military leaders serve their <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t226-v2\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">king</data> like a servant serves their master. Though these officials and leaders have a high position in the community, the king has a higher position, just like a master has a higher position in the community than a servant does. Some translators translate these people's roles as \"official\" or \"military leader\" instead of servant.</p>",
        "textSha256": "f156f59ba7c5681854580171332411b1325af656773f50e2d8b943f4abbe485d",
        "provenance": {
          "status": "source",
